      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.17,
            0.12
          ],
          "content": "CIRCLES"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.863,
            0.104,
            0.898,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "147"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.138,
            0.709,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": "**Take a point Q on XY other than P and join OQ (see Fig. 10.5).**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.164,
            0.562,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": "**The point Q must lie outside the circle. (Why? Note that if Q lies inside the circle, XY will become a secant and not a tangent to the circle). Therefore, OQ is longer than the radius OP of the circle. That is,**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.288,
            0.261,
            0.399,
            0.279
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm { O Q } > \\mathrm { O P } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.285,
            0.563,
            0.383
          ],
          "content": "**Since this happens for every point on the line XY except the point P, OP is the shortest of all the distances of the point O to the points of XY. So OP is perpendicular to XY. (as shown in Theorem A1.7.)**"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.586,
            0.188,
            0.882,
            0.35
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.678,
                0.358,
                0.778,
                0.377
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 10.5"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.414,
            0.202,
            0.433
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Remarks"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.44,
            0.901,
            0.479
          ],
          "content": "**1. By theorem above, we can also conclude that at any point on a circle there can be one and only one tangent.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.484,
            0.9,
            0.523
          ],
          "content": "**2. The line containing the radius through the point of contact is also sometimes called the ‘normal’ to the circle at the point.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.402,
            0.542,
            0.595,
            0.563
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "EXERCISE 10.1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.573,
            0.495,
            0.591
          ],
          "content": "**1. How many tangents can a circle have?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.596,
            0.315,
            0.614
          ],
          "content": "**2. Fill in the blanks :**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.62,
            0.718,
            0.638
          ],
          "content": "**(i) A tangent to a circle intersects it in point (s).**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.643,
            0.774,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": "**(ii) A line intersecting a circle in two points is called a**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.666,
            0.735,
            0.685
          ],
          "content": "**(iii) A circle can have parallel tangents at the most.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.69,
            0.896,
            0.708
          ],
          "content": "**(iv) The common point of a tangent to a circle and the circle is called**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.713,
            0.9,
            0.778
          ],
          "content": "**3. A tangent PQ at a point P of a circle of radius 5 cm meets a line through the centre O at a point Q so that OQ = 12 cm. Length PQ is : (A) 12 cm (B) 13 cm (C) 8.5 cm (D)** $\\left[ { \\sqrt { 1 1 9 } } { \\mathrm { ~ c m . } } \\right.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.781,
            0.9,
            0.816
          ],
          "content": "**4. Draw a circle and two lines parallel to a given line such that one is a tangent and the other, a secant to the circle.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.829,
            0.64,
            0.849
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "10.3 Number of Tangents from a Point on a Circle"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.855,
            0.901,
            0.895
          ],
          "content": "**To get an idea of the number of tangents from a point on a circle, let us perform the following activity:**"
        },
